{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean head 986ac393fe7b4f531440c70031009e42069c1b33, artifact cdb1e0d58334386ab7c39068ff43709a64dfb944.\n\nArchitecture. The affected layers are the kernel (internal/kernel), the Workroom application (internal/app), the service surface (internal/service) and the browser (ui/). This head preserves the kernel-application contract. The kernel's stated job is to prove who signed opaque bytes, which sequence admitted them and where they stand; Progress and AuditProgress add an observation channel beside that and change nothing about it. The types are documented as reporting-only and the code matches the comment: nothing in the kernel reads them back, no verification decision consults them, and the existing Load signature is unchanged, with the progress-carrying variant added alongside and nil threaded through the pre-existing scanHead call sites. The kernel still understands no application meaning. No update to docs/reference/architecture.md is owed. That page does not exist at this head, because the head predates the commit on main that added it; I read it from main. That is not a defect in the head, and the head merges clean over it.\n\nSecurity. Cancellation churn is the point of the change and it holds. snapshotFlight runs the audit on context.Background() in a goroutine, so a caller's cancellation cannot abort verification, and the flight is cleared under flightMu only when it is still the current one, so a finishing flight cannot evict its successor. The single-flight test asserts the property rather than the mechanism: after the first reader cancels mid-audit, Verified must never regress and Total must never change, which is exactly what a restarted or aborted rebuild would violate.\n\nProjection secrecy holds, and is tested adversarially. /v0/rebuild serves running, verified and total, and the test reads the endpoint into a bare map and fails on any key outside those three, so a future field cannot leak in unnoticed. Serving no projection during a rebuild is the right call on a fold-profile change, where the previous checkpoint is invalid under a contract this binary no longer implements: there is no verified projection to show, and progress with no work data is the honest answer. A third reader with a short deadline receives a deadline error rather than a partial or stale projection, which is the atomic-readiness claim, tested.\n\nProgress bounds are counters only. Races: go test -race is silent on internal/app, internal/kernel and internal/service at this head, and AuditProgress carries atomic.Bool and atomic.Int64 throughout, so the lock-free read in RebuildProgress is sound. Snapshot loads the three fields independently, so Verified and Total may come from different instants; the comment claims only coherent-enough monotonic observation, which is the correct claim for a bounded display and not for a verification input.\n\nNo new exposure. The resident API carries no authentication at all and /v0/status already serves the entire projection to any loopback reader, so three counters are strictly less than what is already available.\n\nSimplification. I looked and found nothing that does not weaken a condition. The doubled select in SnapshotWithSource is worth keeping: without the leading non-blocking select, Go chooses at random when both done and ctx.Done() are ready, so a caller whose context expired exactly as the answer arrived would sometimes discard a completed verified snapshot. Holding both flightMu and an atomic.Pointer looks redundant until you notice that the atomic load is what lets RebuildProgress answer without taking any lock, which is the whole reason the endpoint exists — the rebuild holds snapshotMu for its duration, so a locking reporter could not report.\n\nReproduced rather than reasoned about. Under -race at this head: TestProfileMismatchRebuildIsSingleFlightAndPublishesAtomically passes (8.58s), forcing a genuine mismatch by signing a checkpoint under a deliberately different profile and requiring a fresh Workspace to reject it and audit from genesis; TestColdAuditReportsItsProgressWhileHoldingTheLock passes (6.46s); TestMatchingCheckpointDoesNotStartColdAuditProgress passes (0.55s), which is the warm-quiet claim. The final projection's frontier matches the independently loaded verification head and depth, and contains the last record written, so the joined reader receives the whole answer and not a prefix.\n\nGates at this exact head, in a detached worktree of my own so as not to disturb the singleflight worktree: go vet silent, go test -race silent on the three changed packages, the full suite passes, make ui-check exits 0 and reproduces the committed embed, the worktree is clean. The head is 6 commits ahead of main and 5 behind, and merges clean.\n\nOne observation, deliberately not a blocker. The artifact records path request/resident-rebuild-singleflight, which is a branch name rather than a repository path, so no merge will ever change a file at that string and nothing can supersede it there. I measured before raising it: 50 of 503 artifacts use branch-like paths, 19 of them live, and my own request/mcp-projection-notes is among them. It is the established shape for a pre-merge artifact pointing at a head for review, and the path discipline in AGENTS.md is written about merge artifacts, so blocking on it would be applying a rule where it was not aimed. It does have a consequence worth carrying to step 5: the merge must publish successors at the strings the areas keep using — internal/app, internal/kernel, internal/service and ui/ — and retire this one with a bare supersede, because nothing will ever reach it by path.\n\nOne question, not a finding. RebuildProgress returns running as progress.Started, so between the flight being created and the kernel calling begin() a reader sees the quiet path. The window looks harmless and the tests cover the observable behaviour either side of it; worth a word only if it was not intended.\n\nMy earlier changes-requested, db811030, was against 986ac393e8cfde1ade1ba9ff98e15f5a44ca5ca3, a head that does not exist. That request was retired and re-filed at this real head, which is the commit I had in fact been reading, so the substance above is the same review completed — with the gates I declined to run while the named head and the read head were different commits.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:cdb1e0d58334386ab7c39068ff43709a64dfb944","head":"986ac393fe7b4f531440c70031009e42069c1b33","verdict":"approved"}}